  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications apply protein language models and learning-to-rank methods to design, kinase, enzyme, and variant prediction tasks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the coupling of learned sequence representations with task-specific prediction or search across protein engineering problems. Three preprints address design and optimisation directly: GLOM-TGITO pairs a global-local multiscale fluorescence predictor with multiview landscape-guided search for ppluGFP2, while projected gradient descent is proposed as an alternative to softmax-reparameterised optimisation for hallucination-based binder design. KINBERT applies a protein language model with phospho-PCA pooled screening and Bayesian hierarchical modelling to kinase-substrate interactions. Published work extends this pattern to enzyme kinetic parameters (SAEKP, with a site attention module) and optimal temperature (AdventML, with transformer embeddings, R2 about 0.64). ESMRank fuses ESM2 embeddings with physicochemical features in a learning-to-rank framework across roughly 1100 DMS datasets. APV-Sankey is a distinct visualization tool for tracking k-mer persistence in HT-SELEX aptamer data.",
